\section*{S4. Actual budgets and output behaviour}

\begin{table}[H]
\centering
\caption{Recorded operating quantities. Tensor elements are processor outputs,
not asserted visual-token counts. Peak memory is observed allocated memory on
the H200 run.}
\scriptsize
\begin{tabular}{p{0.31\linewidth}rrrrrr}
\toprule
Condition & Actual input & Cap & Tokens mean & Tokens p95 & Cap rate & Peak GiB \\
\midrule
@@OPERATING_ROWS@@
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

For the Qwen 512-token value control, processor tensor elements are 2,211,840
at 768 and 35,979,264 at 3072, a 16.27-fold ratio. The 768 condition generates
substantially longer outputs and reaches the cap more often. This is why the
observed end-to-end latency is not interpreted as visual encoding cost. Legacy
192-token Qwen rows record resized image dimensions and output lengths but do
not expose processor tensor elements or peak allocation; their input tensor
sizes are not back-filled as if recorded.

\section*{S5. Cross-family ladder and OCR comparator}

InternVL3.5-8B uses the same 100 value records and identical source
derangement as Qwen E3. Each image condition realises seven tiles (4,214,784
tensor elements), and the no-image condition passes no pixel tensor. All three
conditions use a 512-token cap. PaddleOCR uses the complete source drawing,
the English PP-OCRv3 detector and PP-OCRv4 recognizer, CPU execution, no crop,
no angle classifier, detector maximum side 3072, and the same frozen legal-tag
grammar. A reference-free postprocessor optionally joins a recognized prefix
fragment to the nearest vertically stacked numeric OCR box within frozen
distance limits; fully formed tags with final numeric segments longer than two
characters are never extended. Literal and geometry-joined scores are both
reported. Neither parser is tuned on Set B answers.

