\section*{S7. Deterministic evidence-case selection}

{\raggedright
The positive Figure 1 rule first requires correct-3072 strict tag F1 $=1$ and
correct-768, shuffled-3072, and no-image strict tag F1 $=0$. It then selects
the lexicographically smallest SHA-256 of
\texttt{source\_id || ``||'' || instance\_id}. There are
@@VALUE_ELIGIBLE@@ eligible records; the selected instance is
\texttt{@@VALUE_INSTANCE@@}, source \texttt{@@VALUE_SOURCE@@}, rank
\texttt{@@VALUE_HASH@@}. A separate archived boundary-case rule requires a spatial-count record
that is strictly correct without an image and strictly wrong with the correct
3072 image, then applies the same hash rank. There are @@STRUCT_ELIGIBLE@@
eligible records; the selected instance is \texttt{@@STRUCT_INSTANCE@@}, source
\texttt{@@STRUCT_SOURCE@@}, rank \texttt{@@STRUCT_HASH@@}. Public records do
not contain tag coordinates for the selected value instance, so no crop or
box is shown. Both source drawings are covered by the local PIDQA CC0 record.
\par}

\section*{S8. Deterministic reproduction and environment}

Run \texttt{python scripts/reproduce\_submission\_v5.py --root .} from the
repository root. It re-scores included immutable outputs, rebuilds analyses and figures,
regenerates manuscript sources, compiles both PDFs, and runs package checks. It
does not rerun model inference or retry the unavailable external-data branch.
The package records Python dependencies and the isolated OCR environment in
machine-readable lock files. Raw JSONL retains model identifiers and per-row
revisions, while release manifests retain SHA-256 values. GPU results were
generated on NVIDIA H200 devices; model weights are not redistributed.

The archival DOI/URL and author-owned administrative declarations cannot be
generated by an automated technical workflow and remain explicit submitter
fields. Their absence is not presented as experimental evidence.

